Pat Rankin said that, but it's incorrect, since
monster extinction _is_ in the game and black
puddings _are_ farm-able; he simply misunderstood the
effect, and perhaps the intent, of adding monster
extinction limits.

Since, before those limits, you could altar camp and
farm _every_ kind of monster [save for uniques and
nazgul/ereynes, which have their own limits], more
than likely the monster extinction limits were put
in to address that larger problem.

As many here have said, and I agree, the "fix" to
remove pudding farming would be a handful of lines
of code, and easy.

If the DevTeam had intended to fix _pudding farming_
at the time _monster extinction limits_ were
installed, they surely could have and would have
done so.

If pudding farming was a live issue at the time,
then NOT doing so is a measure of DevTeam attitude,
but I have no evidence either way on that issue.

It is not too late to find some, though, since
Google Groups' archives are working today.

Yes, pudding farming was well known, and had that
name, back when the last NetHack release was still a
glimmer in the eye of the DevTeam.

Here's Dylan, commenting on pudding farming by that
very name just over TWO FULL YEARS before the most
recent NetHack release:

http://groups.google.com/group/rec.games.roguelike.nethack/msg/ef2eaf1918788467?dmode=source

Thus, the reasonable inference is that the DevTeam
was perfectly well aware of pudding farming, and left
pudding farming in place _intentionally_, in NetHack
3.4.3, despite Pat Rankin's understanding to the
contrary.

Whether the DevTeam will follow that precedent in
the next release, with all the fury pudding farming
generates today, both pro and con, is Not My Call, I
am sure, I just want my preferences that it stay,
well known.

